yes(643,'D','Hydrogen chloride dissolves to supply chloride ions. These react with silver ions to form white silver chloride: Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl(s). Nitric-acid acidification suppresses interference from carbonate and sulfite precipitates.',[RX],question='Which gas forms a white precipitate when passed into aqueous silver nitrate acidified with nitric acid?',options=['NH₃','SO₂','CO₂','HCl'],audit='Standardized silver nitrate name and made acidification reagent explicit for the conventional halide test.')
yes(644,'A','All three halogens react with aqueous alkalis. For example, chlorine disproportionates in cold dilute alkali to chloride and hypochlorite. They are not all liquids, and halogen displacement works in the direction of decreasing oxidising strength rather than mutually in both directions.',[REB],question='Chlorine, bromine and iodine resemble one another in that they',options=['Dissolve in alkalis','React violently with hydrogen without heating','Are liquids','Displace one another from solutions of their salts'],audit='Corrected oldD→A; mutual displacement claim is false.')
yes(645,'B','Sodium sulfite reacts with acid to release sulfur dioxide: Na₂SO₃ + 2HCl → 2NaCl + H₂O + SO₂. Its pungent gas reduces purple permanganate to Mn²⁺. Sodium sulfide instead gives hydrogen sulfide, characterised by a rotten-egg smell.',[RX,'https://myschool.ng/classroom/chemistry/1465?page=15'],question='Which salt reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid to produce a pungent-smelling gas that decolourises acidified purple potassium permanganate solution?',options=['Na₂SO₄','Na₂SO₃','Na₂S','Na₂CO₃'],audit='Recovered complete pungent-smelling premise from fully opened matching Myschool1465, all four options checked.',check='sulfite-acid')
yes(646,'A','Hydrogen combustion in oxygen releases substantial energy: 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. The resulting high-temperature flame provides heat for melting metals; low gas density is not the reason.',[RX],question='Hydrogen is used in oxyhydrogen flames for melting metals because it',options=['Evolves a lot of heat when burnt','Combines explosively with oxygen','Is a very light gas','Is a rocket fuel'])
yes(647,'A','Heating ammonium chloride with calcium hydroxide releases ammonia: 2NH₄Cl + Ca(OH)₂ → CaCl₂ + 2NH₃ + 2H₂O. Calcium oxide dries the ammonia without reacting with it as an acidic drying agent would.',[REB,'https://myschool.ng/classroom/chemistry/31569?filter=oldest','https://www.elearning.rtb.gov.rw/mod/book/tool/print/index.php?id=1026'],question='A heated flask contains mixture Y. Gas passes from the flask through a drying vessel containing calcium oxide, then into an inverted gas jar labelled ammonia. What is mixture Y?',options=['Calcium hydroxide and ammonium chloride','Calcium hydroxide and sodium chloride','Sodium chloride and ammonium nitrate','Sodium nitrite and ammonium chloride'],audit='PDF56 visually inspected: heatedYflask, CaOdryer, inverted ammoniajar fully described. Readable Myschool31569 restores optionB sodiumchloride without false(V). Removed old unsupported endothermic claim.',check='ammonia-preparation')
yes(648,'C','Duralumin is an aluminium alloy that combines low density with greater strength than pure aluminium. That strength-to-weight advantage makes it useful for structures where mass matters.',[REB],question='Which properties make duralumin especially useful compared with its constituent metals?',options=['Heavy with a high melting point','Malleable with high density','Strong and light','Hard and ductile'])
yes(649,'B','Magnesium and calcium are highly reactive metals for which electrolysis of molten compounds is an established extraction route. Zinc, copper and lead can be obtained by chemical reduction routes instead.',[EL,REB],question='Which listed pair of reactive metals is extracted by electrolysis of suitable molten compounds?',options=['Magnesium and zinc','Magnesium and calcium','Copper and zinc','Lead and calcium'],audit='Clarified ores require suitable molten compounds rather than direct electrolysis of raw rock; no claim electrolysis is the only modern industrial route.')
yes(650,'A','Concentrated nitric acid can make iron passive by producing a protective surface film that greatly slows further attack. This is the standard iron passivation example.',[REB,'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B9780444422521500540'],question='Which listed metal becomes passive in concentrated nitric acid?',options=['Iron','Tin','Copper','Zinc'],audit='Standardized acidname. Independent passivation support in indexed primary1983symposium abstract; publisherfullpage403 so not claimed fully accessible.')
