yes(657,'C','Ethyl ethanoate is CH₃–C(=O)–O–C₂H₅. Swapping the terminal methyl and ethyl groups gives C₂H₅–C(=O)–O–CH₃. The alcohol-derived group is methyl and the acid-derived part has three carbons, so the ester is methyl propanoate.',[ORG,'https://myschool.ng/classroom/chemistry/1480?c_page=1&filter=oldest&page=27'],question='When the two terminal alkyl groups of ethyl ethanoate are interchanged, the compound formed is',options=['Methyl ethanoate','Ethyl propanoate','Methyl propanoate','Propyl ethanoate'],audit='Full matching Myschool1480 confirms allfour names, including garbledC. Corrected B→C without changing totalcarboncount.',check='ester-swap')
